  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A pair of cold fronts will traverse ENC over the \nnext couple of days as an unusually strong upper level trough \ndevelops over the Eastern U.S. The first cold front is currently \nmoving through central NC. This front will move across ENC between \n4pm-10pm. Recent radar imagery shows scattered, high-based showers \ndeveloping along the front, and hi-res guidance suggests some of \nthis activity may make it into ENC this evening. While low-level \nmoisture is lacking, modest mid-level moisture, weak instability, \nand strong forcing along the front appears to support this idea. In \nlight of the potential, the chance of showers was slightly increased \nover the next several hours. No lightning has been observed yet, but \na few strikes cannot be ruled out. Breezy southwest winds will \ncontinue for a few more hours ahead of the front, then a thump of \nwinds behind the front is possible. Winds were increased for a few \nhours just behind the front to account for the enhanced mixing as \nstrong CAA ensues.\n\nThe strong CAA will usher in a noticeably colder airmass compared to \nwhat we have had the past couple of days. This initial front will \nknock highs down into the 50s and 60s, with lows in the 40s and 50s. \nThe second front, which will move through late Friday, will \nreinforce the cold airmass. In the wake of that front, highs will \nonly manage to reach the 50s, while lows tumble into the 20s and 30s \ninland, and 30s and 40s along the coast. There was some \nconsideration for a Freeze Watch for a part of the area Friday \nnight. However, even the 10th percentile of guidance keeps most of \nthe area above 32 degrees. While a few sheltered areas may fall \nbelow freezing, the risk of freezing temperatures looks more \nmarginal Friday night. Additionally, while some areas will fall into \nthe low to mid 30s, there should be enough wind to prevent frost \ndevelopment. Therefore, no frost/freeze headlines are planned for \nthat time period. Looking ahead, there is a much better chance of \nfrost/freeze conditions Saturday night and Sunday night as a strong \nsurface high builds in and winds decrease. Headlines will likely be \nneeded at some point this weekend, and potentially into early next \nweek.",
